WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:06.520
AI can help explain vulnerability classes and organize reasoning, but it should not turn suggestions into findings.

00:00:06.740 --> 00:00:10.980
In web assessment, the same symptom can have different meanings depending on context.

00:00:11.200 --> 00:00:19.120
An error message, unusual response, missing field check, or role mismatch may be important, harmless, or misunderstood.

00:00:19.340 --> 00:00:23.580
AI can help the analyst form hypotheses, but evidence confirms the issue.

00:00:23.800 --> 00:00:34.760
Input validation, access control, session management, business logic, insecure configuration, and dependency risk are common areas where AI can provide educational support.

00:00:34.980 --> 00:00:43.620
For example, it can explain what kinds of evidence usually matter for an access control concern or help compare an observation against a known vulnerability category.

00:00:43.840 --> 00:00:49.040
It should not be asked to perform unauthorized exploitation or make assumptions beyond the data provided.

00:00:49.260 --> 00:00:52.300
Business logic issues are a good reminder of AI limits.

00:00:52.520 --> 00:00:56.920
These findings often depend on how the organization expects the application to work.

00:00:57.140 --> 00:01:01.380
A sequence that looks suspicious may be normal for a privileged workflow.

00:01:01.600 --> 00:01:04.320
A missing check may be enforced elsewhere.

00:01:04.540 --> 00:01:12.140
The assistant may help identify questions to ask, but the analyst must understand the application and verify behavior with authorized evidence.

00:01:12.360 --> 00:01:14.840
Treat AI output as advisory.

00:01:15.060 --> 00:01:26.740
Useful prompts ask the assistant to separate facts from assumptions, list missing evidence, suggest safe verification questions, or draft a finding only after the analyst provides confirmed observations.

00:01:26.960 --> 00:01:29.280
This workflow encourages disciplined reasoning.

00:01:29.500 --> 00:01:35.180
It also reduces the risk that confident language turns an unverified idea into a reportable claim.
